{
  "diagnostics": [],
  "file_attributes": [
    {
      "doc_text": " # The Rust Core Library",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        0,
        27
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        28,
        31
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " The Rust Core Library is the dependency-free[^free] foundation of [The",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        32,
        106
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " Rust Standard Library](../std/index.html). It is the portable glue",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        107,
        177
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " between the language and its libraries, defining the intrinsic and",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        178,
        248
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " primitive building blocks of all Rust code. It links to no",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        249,
        311
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " upstream libraries, no system libraries, and no libc.",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        312,
        369
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        370,
        373
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " [^free]: Strictly speaking, there are some symbols which are needed but",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        374,
        449
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "          they aren't always necessary.",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        450,
        492
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        493,
        496
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " The core library is *minimal*: it isn't even aware of heap allocation,",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        497,
        571
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " nor does it provide concurrency or I/O. These things require",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        572,
        636
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " platform integration, and this library is platform-agnostic.",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        637,
        701
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        702,
        705
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " # How to use the core library",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        706,
        739
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        740,
        743
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " Please note that all of these details are currently not considered stable.",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        744,
        822
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        823,
        826
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " This library is built on the assumption of a few existing symbols:",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        892,
        962
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        963,
        966
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " * `memcpy`, `memmove`, `memset`, `memcmp`, `bcmp`, `strlen` - These are core memory routines",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        967,
        1063
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "   which are generated by Rust codegen backends. Additionally, this library can make explicit",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1064,
        1160
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "   calls to `strlen`. Their signatures are the same as found in C, but there are extra",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1161,
        1250
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "   assumptions about their semantics: For `memcpy`, `memmove`, `memset`, `memcmp`, and `bcmp`, if",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1251,
        1351
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "   the `n` parameter is 0, the function is assumed to not be UB, even if the pointers are NULL or",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1352,
        1452
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "   dangling. (Note that making extra assumptions about these functions is common among compilers:",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1453,
        1553
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "   [clang](https://reviews.llvm.org/D86993) and [GCC](https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Standards.html#C-Language) do the same.)",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1554,
        1686
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "   These functions are often provided by the system libc, but can also be provided by the",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1687,
        1779
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "   [compiler-builtins crate](https://crates.io/crates/compiler_builtins).",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1780,
        1856
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "   Note that the library does not guarantee that it will always make these assumptions, so Rust",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1857,
        1955
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "   user code directly calling the C functions should follow the C specification! The advice for",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        1956,
        2054
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "   Rust user code is to call the functions provided by this library instead (such as",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2055,
        2142
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "   `ptr::copy`).",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2143,
        2162
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2163,
        2166
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " * Panic handler - This function takes one argument, a `&panic::PanicInfo`. It is up to consumers of this core",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2167,
        2280
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "   library to define this panic function; it is only required to never",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2281,
        2354
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "   return. You should mark your implementation using `#[panic_handler]`.",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2355,
        2430
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2431,
        2434
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": " * `rust_eh_personality` - is used by the failure mechanisms of the",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2435,
        2505
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "   compiler. This is often mapped to GCC's personality function, but crates",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2506,
        2584
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "   which do not trigger a panic can be assured that this function is never",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2585,
        2662
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
    {
      "doc_text": "   called. The `lang` attribute is called `eh_personality`.",
      "path": "doc",
      "range": [
        2663,
        2725
      ],
      "style": "inner"
    },
